%% chapter-17.ltx — Names, Binding, and the Environment Model
%% Part II: Programming Language Design

%% ============================================================
%% 17.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:names-binding-intro}

Names are the primary means by which programmers refer to computational
entities: variables, functions, types, modules, labels.  The rules that
govern how names are associated with the entities they denote are among the
most fundamental aspects of any programming language.

In this chapter, we study the theory of names and binding.  We begin with
the concept of \emph{name resolution}---how a name occurrence is connected
to its declaration.  We then examine \emph{binding time}---when the
association between a name and its entity is established.  We develop the
\emph{environment model} of evaluation, which provides a formal framework
for understanding how names are resolved during program execution.  We
conclude with the implementation of symbol tables and the treatment of
overloading and name mangling.

\begin{definition}[Binding]\label{def:binding}
  A \emph{binding} is an association between a name (identifier) and the
  entity it denotes (a variable, function, type, module, etc.).  The
  binding is established by a \emph{declaration} and used by
  \emph{references} (occurrences of the name).
\end{definition}

%% ============================================================
%% 17.2 Name Resolution
%% ============================================================
\section{Name Resolution}\label{sec:name-resolution}

\subsection{The Name Resolution Problem}\label{sec:name-resolution-problem}

\begin{definition}[Name Resolution]\label{def:name-resolution}
  \emph{Name resolution} is the process of determining, for each occurrence
  of a name in a program, which declaration it refers to.  This is also
  called \emph{identifier resolution} or \emph{binding resolution}.
\end{definition}

Name resolution is a prerequisite for all other phases of compilation.
Before the compiler can type-check, optimize, or generate code for an
expression, it must know what each name refers to.

\begin{example}[Name Resolution]\label{ex:name-resolution}
  Consider:
  \begin{verbatim}
  let x = 1 in
  let f = fun y -> x + y in
  let x = 2 in
  f x
  \end{verbatim}
  Name resolution must determine:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item The \texttt{x} in \texttt{x + y} refers to the first \texttt{x} (value 1).
    \item The \texttt{x} in \texttt{f x} refers to the second \texttt{x} (value 2).
    \item The \texttt{y} in \texttt{x + y} refers to the parameter \texttt{y}.
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Scope and Binding}\label{sec:scope-binding}

\begin{definition}[Scope]\label{def:scope}
  The \emph{scope} of a declaration is the region of the program in which
  the binding established by the declaration is visible.  A name occurrence
  is \emph{bound} if it falls within the scope of a declaration; otherwise,
  it is \emph{free} (or \emph{unbound}).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Binding Time]\label{def:binding-time}
  The \emph{binding time} is the point during the program lifecycle when a
  binding is established:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Language design time}: The meaning of keywords is fixed.
    \item \textbf{Compile time}: Types, static addresses, and optimizations.
    \item \textbf{Link time}: Resolution of external references.
    \item \textbf{Load time}: Dynamic library loading.
    \item \textbf{Run time}: Dynamic typing, dynamic binding, reflection.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Static vs. Dynamic Scoping}\label{sec:static-vs-dynamic-scoping}

\begin{definition}[Static (Lexical) Scoping]\label{def:static-scoping}
  Under \emph{static scoping}, the binding of a name is determined by the
  program text (the lexical structure).  A name refers to the declaration
  in the nearest enclosing scope in the source code.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dynamic Scoping]\label{def:dynamic-scoping}
  Under \emph{dynamic scoping}, the binding of a name is determined by the
  call stack at runtime.  A name refers to the most recent binding
  established by a caller in the dynamic call chain.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Static vs. Dynamic Scoping]\label{ex:static-dynamic-scoping}
  \begin{verbatim}
  let x = 1
  let f = fun () -> x
  let g = fun () -> let x = 2 in f ()
  \end{verbatim}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item With \emph{static scoping}: \texttt{g()} returns 1 (the \texttt{x}
      in \texttt{f} refers to the global \texttt{x}).
    \item With \emph{dynamic scoping}: \texttt{g()} returns 2 (the \texttt{x}
      in \texttt{f} refers to the \texttt{x} bound by \texttt{g}).
  \end{itemize}
\end{example}

Most modern languages use static scoping.  Dynamic scoping is found in
Emacs Lisp, some shell scripting languages, and as a special feature in
Common Lisp (\texttt{special} variables).

\subsection{Name Resolution Rules}\label{sec:name-resolution-rules}

The rules for name resolution vary across languages:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Single-pass}: Names must be declared before use (C, Pascal).
  \item \textbf{Two-pass}: Names can be used before declaration (Java, C\#).
  \item \textbf{Block-scoped}: Names are visible within their enclosing
    block (C99, Java, Rust).
  \item \textbf{Function-scoped}: Names are visible throughout their
    enclosing function (JavaScript \texttt{var}).
  \item \textbf{Hoisting}: Declarations are moved to the top of their scope
    (JavaScript \texttt{var} and \texttt{function}).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 17.3 Binding Time and Binding Rules
%% ============================================================
\section{Binding Time and Binding Rules}\label{sec:binding-time-rules}

\subsection{Early vs. Late Binding}\label{sec:early-vs-late}

\begin{definition}[Early Binding]\label{def:early-binding}
  \emph{Early binding} (static binding) resolves names at compile time or
  link time.  The binding is fixed before the program runs.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Late Binding]\label{def:late-binding}
  \emph{Late binding} (dynamic binding) resolves names at runtime.  The
  binding can change during execution.
\end{definition}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Binding Time Examples}
  \label{tab:binding-time}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Binding} & \textbf{Time} & \textbf{Example} \\
    \hline
    Keyword meaning & Design time & \texttt{if} means conditional \\
    Type of variable & Compile time & \texttt{int x} \\
    Function address & Link time & External function call \\
    Method dispatch & Run time & Virtual method call \\
    Variable value & Run time & Assignment \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{The Binding Spectrum}\label{sec:binding-spectrum}

Languages exist on a spectrum from early to late binding:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Very early}: Haskell, Agda---almost everything is resolved
    at compile time.
  \item \textbf{Early}: C, Rust---types and addresses are fixed at compile
    time.
  \item \textbf{Mixed}: Java, C\#---types are checked at compile time, but
    method dispatch is dynamic.
  \item \textbf{Late}: Python, Ruby---most bindings are resolved at runtime.
  \item \textbf{Very late}: Lisp, Smalltalk---almost everything is dynamic.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
Earlier binding enables more optimization and earlier error detection, but
reduces flexibility.  Later binding enables more flexibility and dynamic
behavior, but makes optimization harder and errors harder to detect.  The
right point on the spectrum depends on the application domain.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 17.4 Static and Dynamic Binding
%% ============================================================
\section{Static and Dynamic Binding}\label{sec:static-dynamic-binding}

\subsection{Static Binding}\label{sec:static-binding-detail}

Static binding is the default in most compiled languages.  Under static
binding:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Variables}: Each variable occurrence is resolved to a
    specific memory location (stack slot, register, or global address).
  \item \textbf{Functions}: Each function call is resolved to a specific
    code address.
  \item \textbf{Types}: Each type annotation is resolved to a specific type.
  \item \textbf{Overloads}: Each overloaded function call is resolved to a
    specific overload.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Dynamic Binding}\label{sec:dynamic-binding-detail}

Dynamic binding defers resolution to runtime:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Virtual methods}: The method to call is determined by the
    runtime type of the receiver.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic dispatch}: In dynamically typed languages, the
    operation to perform is determined by the runtime types of the
    arguments.
  \item \textbf{Dynamic loading}: Modules and libraries can be loaded at
    runtime, and their bindings resolved dynamically.
  \item \textbf{Reflection}: Programs can inspect and modify their own
    bindings at runtime.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Implementation of Dynamic Binding}\label{sec:dynamic-binding-impl}

Dynamic binding is typically implemented through:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Virtual method tables (vtables)}: Each object contains a
    pointer to a table of function pointers; method calls go through the
    table.
  \item \textbf{Inline caching}: The first time a method is called on an
    object of a given type, the method address is cached; subsequent calls
    check the cache.
  \item \textbf{Hash tables}: In highly dynamic languages, method lookup is
    performed via hash table search.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 17.5 Environment Model of Evaluation
%% ============================================================
\section{The Environment Model of Evaluation}\label{sec:environment-model}

The environment model provides a formal framework for understanding how
names are resolved during program evaluation.  It is the semantic foundation
for both static and dynamic scoping.

\subsection{Environments}\label{sec:environments}

\begin{definition}[Environment]\label{def:environment}
  An \emph{environment} is a finite mapping from names to values (or
  locations):
  \[
    \rho \in \Env = \Name \finfun \Val
  \]
  We write $\rho[x \mapsto v]$ for the environment that extends $\rho$ with
  the binding $x \mapsto v$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Environment Lookup]\label{def:env-lookup}
  The lookup of name $x$ in environment $\rho$ is:
  \[
    \rho(x) = \begin{cases}
      v & \text{if } \rho = \rho'[x \mapsto v] \\
      \rho'(x) & \text{if } \rho = \rho'[y \mapsto v] \text{ and } y \neq x \\
      \texttt{unbound} & \text{if } x \notin \mathrm{dom}(\rho)
    \end{cases}
  \]
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Environment Model for a Simple Language}\label{sec:env-model-simple}

Consider a simple language with variables, let-bindings, functions, and
application:

\begin{grammar}{Simple Language}
  \production{e}{x \alt n \alt e_1 + e_2 \alt \mathbf{let}\ x = e_1\ \mathbf{in}\ e_2 \alt \mathbf{fun}\ x \to e \alt e_1\ e_2}
\end{grammar}

The evaluation relation is $\bconfig{e, \rho} \evalto v$, meaning
``expression $e$ in environment $\rho$ evaluates to value $v$'':

\begin{semrules}{Environment Model}
  \semrule{
    \rho(x) = v
  }{
    \bconfig{x, \rho} \evalto v
  }{\text{(Var)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \bconfig{e_1, \rho} \evalto v_1 \quad
    \bconfig{e_2, \rho} \evalto v_2
  }{
    \bconfig{e_1 + e_2, \rho} \evalto v_1 + v_2
  }{\text{(Add)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \bconfig{e_1, \rho} \evalto v_1 \quad
    \bconfig{e_2, \rho[v_1/x]} \evalto v
  }{
    \bconfig{\mathbf{let}\ x = e_1\ \mathbf{in}\ e_2, \rho} \evalto v
  }{\text{(Let)}} \\[6pt]
  \semruleaxiom{
    \bconfig{\mathbf{fun}\ x \to e, \rho} \evalto \langle \mathbf{fun}\ x \to e, \rho \rangle
  }{\text{(Fun)}} \\[6pt]
  \semrule{
    \bconfig{e_1, \rho} \evalto \langle \mathbf{fun}\ x \to e, \rho' \rangle \quad
    \bconfig{e_2, \rho} \evalto v_2 \quad
    \bconfig{e, \rho'[x \mapsto v_2]} \evalto v
  }{
    \bconfig{e_1\ e_2, \rho} \evalto v
  }{\text{(App)}}
\end{semrules}

\begin{insight}
The environment model captures static scoping naturally: when a function is
applied, it uses the environment $\rho'$ that was captured when the function
was \emph{created}, not the environment $\rho$ of the caller.  This is the
essence of lexical scoping.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Closures}\label{sec:closures-env}

\begin{definition}[Closure]\label{def:closure-env}
  A \emph{closure} is a pair $\langle e, \rho \rangle$ consisting of a
  function body $e$ and an environment $\rho$ that captures the bindings
  visible at the point where the function was defined.
\end{definition}

Closures are the mechanism by which static scoping is implemented.  When a
function is created, the current environment is captured; when the function
is applied, the captured environment is used to resolve names in the
function body.

\subsection{Dynamic Scoping in the Environment Model}\label{sec:dynamic-env}

Under dynamic scoping, function application uses the \emph{caller's}
environment rather than the captured environment:

\begin{semrules}{Dynamic Scoping}
  \semrule{
    \bconfig{e_1, \rho} \evalto \mathbf{fun}\ x \to e \quad
    \bconfig{e_2, \rho} \evalto v_2 \quad
    \bconfig{e, \rho[x \mapsto v_2]} \evalto v
  }{
    \bconfig{e_1\ e_2, \rho} \evalto v
  }{\text{(Dyn-App)}}
\end{semrules}

Note the difference: in the static rule, the body $e$ is evaluated in
$\rho'[x \mapsto v_2]$ where $\rho'$ is the captured environment; in the
dynamic rule, it is evaluated in $\rho[x \mapsto v_2]$ where $\rho$ is the
caller's environment.

%% ============================================================
%% 17.6 Symbol Tables and Their Implementation
%% ============================================================
\section{Symbol Tables and Their Implementation}\label{sec:symbol-tables}

\subsection{Symbol Table Abstract Data Type}\label{sec:symbol-table-adt}

\begin{definition}[Symbol Table]\label{def:symbol-table}
  A \emph{symbol table} is a data structure that records information about
  declarations.  It supports the following operations:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item $\texttt{enter\_scope}()$: Begin a new scope.
    \item $\texttt{exit\_scope}()$: End the current scope.
    \item $\texttt{insert}(name, info)$: Record a declaration.
    \item $\texttt{lookup}(name)$: Find the declaration of a name.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Implementation Strategies}\label{sec:symbol-table-impl}

\subsubsection{Linear List}

The simplest implementation is a linked list of (name, info) pairs.  Lookup
is $O(n)$; insertion is $O(1)$.  Suitable for small programs or as a
pedagogical example.

\subsubsection{Hash Table per Scope}

Each scope has its own hash table.  Scopes are linked in a chain (the
\emph{scope stack}).  Lookup traverses the chain from innermost to
outermost scope:

\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{Lookup}{$name$}
  \For{each scope $s$ from innermost to outermost}
    \If{$name \in s.\texttt{table}$}
      \State \Return $s.\texttt{table}[name]$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State \Return \texttt{not found}
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}

Average lookup is $O(1)$ per scope; worst case is $O(d)$ where $d$ is the
nesting depth.

\subsubsection{Tree-Based Symbol Tables}

A tree structure mirrors the block structure of the program.  Each node
represents a scope and contains a hash table of local declarations plus
pointers to parent and child scopes.

\subsubsection{Fully Persistent Data Structures}

For languages with first-class environments (e.g., Scheme), symbol tables
must support persistence---the ability to create new versions without
destroying old ones.  Persistent hash maps (e.g., hash array mapped tries)
provide $O(\log n)$ lookup and update with structural sharing.

\subsection{Symbol Table Contents}\label{sec:symbol-table-contents}

A symbol table entry typically records:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Name}: The identifier string.
  \item \textbf{Kind}: Variable, function, type, module, label, etc.
  \item \textbf{Type}: The declared or inferred type.
  \item \textbf{Scope level}: The nesting depth.
  \item \textbf{Storage}: Stack offset, register, global address, etc.
  \item \textbf{Attributes}: \texttt{const}, \texttt{static},
    \texttt{mutable}, visibility, etc.
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 17.7 Overloading and Name Mangling
%% ============================================================
\section{Overloading and Name Mangling}\label{sec:overloading-mangling}

\subsection{Function Overloading}\label{sec:function-overloading}

\begin{definition}[Overloading]\label{def:overloading}
  \emph{Function overloading} allows multiple functions with the same name
  but different parameter types to coexist.  The compiler selects the
  appropriate function based on the types of the arguments.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Function Overloading in C++]\label{ex:overloading-cpp}
  \begin{verbatim}
  int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
  double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }
  string add(string a, string b) { return a + b; }
  \end{verbatim}
  The call \texttt{add(1, 2)} resolves to the first overload;
  \texttt{add(1.0, 2.0)} resolves to the second.
\end{example}

\subsection{Overload Resolution}\label{sec:overload-resolution}

Overload resolution is the process of selecting the best matching overload:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Exact match}: The argument types exactly match the parameter
    types.
  \item \textbf{Promotion}: The argument types can be promoted (e.g.,
    \texttt{int} to \texttt{long}).
  \item \textbf{Conversion}: The argument types can be converted (e.g.,
    \texttt{int} to \texttt{double}).
  \item \textbf{User-defined conversion}: A user-defined conversion function
    applies.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{definition}[Ambiguous Overload]\label{def:ambiguous-overload}
  An overloaded call is \emph{ambiguous} if no single overload is better
  than all others.  This is a compile-time error.
\end{definition}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Keeping Incomparable Overloads Ambiguous}

The conversion that is best for one argument need not be best for
another. A simple overload policy compares candidates component by
component rather than summing conversion costs. Assume name lookup
has already produced distinct candidate declarations for the same
arity, and each viable implicit conversion has an integer rank,
with smaller ranks preferred. A missing conversion makes that
candidate nonviable. Define $r\prec s$ to mean that $r$ is no worse
at every position and strictly better at some position. The resolver
returns the unique undominated declaration or diagnoses failure;
equal rank vectors from distinct declarations are not merged.

\begin{sudocode}[Select a unique undominated declaration]
\Function{Resolve}{$A,C$}
  \State $V\gets$ empty list
  \ForAll{$f\in C$}
    \State $r\gets\Call{Ranks}{A,f}$
    \If{all entries of $r$ exist}
      \State Append $(f,r)$ to $V$ \Comment{Viable candidates only}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State $M\gets$ empty list
  \ForAll{$(f,r)\in V$}
    \If{no $(g,s)\in V$ satisfies $s\prec r$}
      \State Append $f$ to $M$ \Comment{Keep ties and trade-offs}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \If{$|M|=1$}
    \State \Return its sole declaration
  \EndIf
  \State \Return no match if $M$ is empty; otherwise ambiguity
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
For actual arguments of types $(\mathrm{Int},\mathrm{Int})$,
suppose rank $0$ denotes exact match, $1$ promotion to
$\mathrm{Long}$, and $2$ conversion to $\mathrm{Float}$.
The candidates with formals $(\mathrm{Int},\mathrm{Float})$
and $(\mathrm{Long},\mathrm{Int})$ score $(0,2)$ and $(1,0)$.
Neither candidate is filtered out: the first wins at position
one and the second at position two. Summing would pick the second
without a rule saying that one advantage compensates for the other.
Add $(\mathrm{Int},\mathrm{Int})$ and its score $(0,0)$ filters out
both; only the new declaration remains.

\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
Every entry appended to $M$ has been compared against the entire
viable set. Since strict dominance is transitive and the set is
finite, a unique undominated candidate dominates every other viable
one. Comparing $m$ viable candidates of arity $k$ naively costs
$O(m^2 k)$ for positive $k$. Real languages can apply additional
tie-breakers for generics or method specificity; the example
implements only the stated conversion policy, not the C++ rules.

\subsection{Name Mangling}\label{sec:name-mangling}

\begin{definition}[Name Mangling]\label{def:name-mangling}
  \emph{Name mangling} (name encoding) is the technique of encoding
  additional information (parameter types, namespace, class) into the
  internal name of a function or variable, so that overloaded functions
  have distinct linker names.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Name Mangling in C++]\label{ex:name-mangling}
  \begin{verbatim}
  int add(int, int)         -> _Z3addii
  double add(double, double) -> _Z3adddd
  void Foo::bar(int)         -> _ZN3Foo3barEi
  \end{verbatim}
  The Itanium C++ ABI encoding scheme encodes the function name, namespace,
  class, and parameter types into a unique string.
\end{example}

\subsection{Operator Overloading}\label{sec:operator-overloading}

\begin{definition}[Operator Overloading]\label{def:operator-overloading}
  \emph{Operator overloading} allows operators (\texttt{+}, \texttt{*},
  \texttt{==}, etc.) to be defined for user-defined types.  The operator
  is resolved based on the types of its operands.
\end{definition}

Operator overloading is supported in C++, Rust, Haskell (through type
classes), Python (through special methods like \texttt{\_\_add\_\_}), and
Scala.

\begin{example}[Operator Overloading in Rust]\label{ex:operator-rust}
  \begin{verbatim}
  use std::ops::Add;
  
  struct Complex { re: f64, im: f64 }
  
  impl Add for Complex {
      type Output = Complex;
      fn add(self, other: Complex) -> Complex {
          Complex {
              re: self.re + other.re,
              im: self.im + other.im,
          }
      }
  }
  \end{verbatim}
  This allows \texttt{c1 + c2} to work for \texttt{Complex} values.
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 17.8 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:names-binding-summary}

Names and binding are the connective tissue of programming languages.  The
rules governing name resolution determine how programs are understood by
both compilers and programmers.

The environment model provides a clean formal framework for understanding
name resolution.  It captures both static and dynamic scoping, and it
reveals the essential role of closures in implementing lexical scoping.

Symbol tables are the compiler's data structure for recording and looking
up bindings.  Their design must balance efficiency (fast lookup) with
expressiveness (supporting overloading, namespaces, modules, and generic
instantiation).

Overloading and name mangling illustrate the interaction between the
source-level view (names) and the implementation-level view (addresses).
The compiler must bridge this gap, resolving overloaded names to specific
implementations and generating unique internal names for the linker.
